title: File metadata fields
tags: [[File tracking]]
kind: reference
aliases: stat mime
summary: The mfr_* fields read from the filesystem — which exist on which platform — and the MIME type reconcile detects from content.

The watcher and reconcile write the stat-derived fields of every tracked file and
directory ([[Reserved fields]] has the full list). Their availability varies:

|!Field |!Linux |!macOS |!Windows |
|`mfr_type`, `mfr_size`, `mfr_mtime` |yes |yes |yes |
|`mfr_btime` |if the filesystem supports it (`statx`) |yes (`st_birthtime`) |yes (`ftCreationTime`) |
|`mfr_permissions`, `mfr_uid`, `mfr_gid` |yes |yes |never written |
|`mfr_inode` |yes |yes |never written |
|`mfr_mount` |yes |yes, without identity |never written |

* The Unix-only fields stay ''unknown'' on Windows; a repository written on Unix and later watched on Windows keeps the Unix values untouched (they are simply not refreshed).
* `mfr_btime` comes through `std::fs::Metadata::created()` and is absent where the filesystem keeps no creation time.
* `mfr_inode` (`st_dev:st_ino`, only when the link count exceeds one — [[What counts as a duplicate]]) comes from the same `stat`, no extra call. Windows exposes no file index through `std`, so there every file counts as its own inode — over-counting reclaimable space rather than under-counting it.
* The hashes are pure content and fully portable ([[File fingerprint]]); `mfr_mount`: [[mfr_mount]].

!! MIME type

`mfr_mime` is detected from content by the pure-Rust `infer` crate (magic bytes) on every
platform — no system `libmagic`. The value is always a standard MIME string; what `infer`
cannot classify from magic bytes (plain text, for one) leaves the field absent.

It is filled by reconcile — full and scoped — for files that lack it (on by default;
`mime: false`, `mf reconcile --no-mime`). It is never recomputed: a content change is
not reflected until the field is cleared.
